## Part B: HTTPS capture

### 5. Username and password

No. I cannot find the username and password in readable form in the HTTPS capture because HTTPS uses TLS encryption to protect the HTTP contents.

### 6. Server Name / SNI

The server name shown in the TLS handshake is:

```text
lab-portal.local
```

### 7. Information still visible

An eavesdropper can still see the client and server IP addresses, connection timing and packet sizes, even though the HTTP contents are encrypted.

The client IP address is 10.10.10.50 and the server IP address is 10.10.10.10.

## Part C: Making sense of it

### 8. Why does HTTP vs HTTPS matter for confidentiality?

HTTP sends data without encryption, while HTTPS encrypts the HTTP contents using TLS.

### 9. Untrusted network

I might use public Wi-Fi in a café. HTTPS protects my login credentials and web traffic from being read by someone monitoring the network, but some metadata such as IP addresses, timing and packet sizes can still be visible.

## What surprised me

What surprised me most was how easily the HTTP capture exposed the username, password and session cookie. In the HTTPS capture, the network packets were still visible, but the HTTP contents were encrypted and could not be read directly.

